INSERT INTO "draw_day" VALUES('2026-09-04',5,3.948787087978882226e+00);
INSERT INTO "groups" VALUES(1,'Chinook salmon size and development','How big Chinook Salmon are at each stage and how fast they get there — smolt and juvenile size-at-age by region, growth rates and what drives them, and the sizes at which the switch to piscivory happens.');
INSERT INTO "groups" VALUES(2,'Juvenile Chinook salmon diet','What Chinook Salmon eat from ocean entry through their first summer at sea — Age-0 forage fish, invertebrates and insects, and how the mix shifts by region and season.');
INSERT INTO "groups" VALUES(3,'Immature and adult Chinook salmon diet','What Chinook Salmon eat once they are large enough to be reliably piscivorous — herring, sand lance, capelin and squid across inside, outside and offshore waters, ocean-age 2+ through the return migration.');
INSERT INTO "groups" VALUES(4,'Predators of Chinook salmon','Who eats Chinook Salmon and how much — the main predators by region (killer whales, pinnipeds, salmon sharks, birds, predatory fish including other Chinook) and the consumption and mortality estimates attached to them.');
INSERT INTO "groups" VALUES(5,'Chinook salmon marine survival','What kills Chinook Salmon at sea besides predators and what makes a year good or bad — early marine survival near the natal estuary, contaminants, size-selectivity, upwelling and El Niño, and the rangewide productivity decline.');
INSERT INTO "groups" VALUES(6,'Chinook salmon life cycle','The shape of a Chinook Salmon life from smolt to spawner — the ocean phases and how long they last, how far and how fast the migrations run, when the spawning run happens, and at what age fish mature.');
INSERT INTO "groups" VALUES(7,'Where Chinook salmon go and why','Why Chinook Salmon end up where they do — cold and deep tolerance versus other Pacific salmon, juveniles actively resisting transport to hold near productive water, and the currents and temperature cues that move them.');
INSERT INTO "groups" VALUES(8,'Chinook salmon evolution and lineage','Where Chinook Salmon came from and how their variation is organized — divergence times from the salmonid tree down to the modern lineages, and why life-history types like stream- and ocean-type are a continuum rather than discrete populations.');
INSERT INTO "groups" VALUES(9,'Chinook salmon stock composition','Whose fish are where — the mix of natal origins in any patch of ocean, how western Alaskan, Asian and Columbia stocks trade dominance by region and season, and how stocks are told apart.');
INSERT INTO "groups" VALUES(15,'Fronts in coastal waters','Fronts as a class — how they are classified, the mechanisms that make each kind, and why a front concentrates nutrients and production.');
INSERT INTO "groups" VALUES(16,'Tides, tidal mixing and internal waves','Tides and what they stir — the tidal constituents, bank gyres and larval retention, and internal waves, lee waves and the slicks their convergences make.');
INSERT INTO "groups" VALUES(17,'Ocean basin circulation','The basin-scale machinery — Hadley cells and the westerlies, western boundary jets, and the warm and cold core rings they shed, including what those rings carry with them.');
INSERT INTO "groups" VALUES(18,'Variability in ocean circulation','What ENSO does to the North Pacific — the current and the Aleutian Low moving, and the consequences for plankton, recruitment and where animals turn up.');
INSERT INTO "groups" VALUES(19,'Light, nutrients and the layered water column','How the open ocean sorts itself vertically and what that does to what lives there — light absorption with depth and wavelength, the nutricline, new versus recycled production, the mixed layer and its diurnal and seasonal thermoclines, the energy stored by mixing, and where zooplankton sit and migrate in all of it.');
INSERT INTO "groups" VALUES(20,'The seasonal production cycle','Why production in the open ocean rises and falls through the year — compensation depth, critical depth and the maths behind them, the Gran effect, the spring bloom and what ends it, species succession within a bloom, summer grazing balance, the autumn bloom, and the deep chlorophyll maximum walking downward as the season runs on.');
INSERT INTO "groups" VALUES(21,'Open-ocean upwelling','Upwelling away from any coast — the equatorial divergence and the plume it makes, the seasonality of the trade winds that drive it, its scale against coastal systems, the Antarctic divergence, and the episodic upwelling that fronts and internal waves produce in mid-ocean.');
INSERT INTO "groups" VALUES(22,'The diffusion limitation and life at low Reynolds number','Why being small in water is hard and what organisms do about it — molecular versus eddy diffusion, the Reynolds and Kolmogoroff numbers, the diffusion limitation of metabolism, sinking and swimming as answers to it and the size at which each starts to pay, and the feeding and sensing tricks copepods and other small animals use where viscosity wins.');
INSERT INTO "groups" VALUES(23,'Flow, drag and boundary layers','What moving water does to the things it moves past — skin and pressure drag, how a boundary layer scales with velocity, geostrophic turbulence at scales larger than the depth, the benthic boundary layer and the sorting and larval settlement that happen in it, and how benthic plants and filter feeders live and die by the current over them.');
INSERT INTO "groups" VALUES(24,'The coastal upwelling machinery','How coastal upwelling works and where — the five major eastern boundary currents, Ekman transport and Bakun''s wind stress calculation, how upwelling is inferred from isopycnals, the poleward undercurrent and the nutrient trap, what makes it vary day to day and with coastal mountains, western-margin and winter exceptions, and what El Niño does to it.');
INSERT INTO "groups" VALUES(25,'Upwelling ecosystems','What coastal upwelling feeds — the clupeid dominance common to all the major systems, zooplankton decoupled from primary production by their slower life cycles and the lack of food limitation that follows, the cross-shelf zonation of a maturing bloom, and where sardine and anchovy spawn given that upwelled water is bad for larvae.');
INSERT INTO "groups" VALUES(26,'Estuaries','Semi-enclosed coastal water where the sea is measurably diluted — the definition, buoyancy-driven estuarine circulation and how the outflowing layer thickens seaward, the salt wedge and its deposition zone, tidally mixed estuaries with no circulation at all, fjords and their sills, and the currents animals ride within them.');
INSERT INTO "groups" VALUES(27,'Coastal mixing and plumes','The contest between tidal mixing and stratification on the shelf, and what fresh water does when it gets out — the Richardson number, the transition zone that switches with the lunar cycle, year-round production over shallow mixed banks, slower zooplankton growth where warming is suppressed, and river versus estuarine plumes, their nutrients and their wandering.');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(1,1,'9 of 17 Evolutionarily Significant Units of Chinook Salmon are listed as endangered or threatened under the U.S. Endangered Species Act','2026-07-04');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(2,1,'Folks suggest that climatic cooling and the subsequent evolution of anadromy was a major catalyst for salmonid speciation','2026-07-04');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(3,1,'The genus Oncorhynchus diverged from Salvelinus in the late Oligocene, approximately 25 Ma. Chinook and Coho diverged from one another 9 Ma. ','2026-07-04');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(4,1,'The major genetic lineages of Chinook salmon are believed to have descended from a common ancestor between 50-100 ka. ','2026-07-04');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(5,1,'While there has been this idea of stream-type vs ocean-type salmon. This really only holds in the upper Columbia. Everywhere else the evidence is quite scant. ','2026-07-04');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(6,1,'Demonstrating parallelism at the DNA sequence level, it is now apparent that similar Chinook Salmon life-history types have evolved by way of different evolutionary pathways','2026-07-04');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(7,1,'In the northeast Pacific Ocean, Chinook Salmon Oncorhynchus tshawytscha populations range from central California (Sacramento River) north through Kotzebue Sound, Alaska and have been infrequently reported along the Arctic shores into Canada including the McKenzie River','2026-07-04');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(8,1,'There has been a general decline in Chinook salmon productivity over the recent decade starting about 2000','2026-07-04');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(9,1,'Chinook salmon near each other tend to have similar life history traits but there''s little in the way of unique populations - more like a steady and windy continuum. ','2026-07-04');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(10,1,'The history of artificial propagation of Chinook Salmon goes back 140 years beginning with the Baird Hatchery on the McCloud River, California.','2026-07-04');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(11,1,'Over the past decade, the aggregate releases of Chinook Salmon in the North Pacific has averaged 254 million per year.','2026-07-04');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(12,1,'Hatchery releases in Alaska are between 8 and 12 million Chinook salmon. In Canada it''s in the tens of millions. In CONUS its more like 200 million. ','2026-07-04');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(13,1,'A coded-wire tag is a piece of micro-wire (0.25 × 1.1 mm) that is etched with an identifying code and is injected into the snout of a fish. The wire is magnetized to enable detection of the wire.','2026-07-04');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(14,1,'The ocean life of Chinook Salmon along the eastern Pacific Rim can be portrayed as occurring in three general phases: 1) a juvenile seaward migration phase after smolting, spanning the first months of ocean life until winter, 2) an immature rearing phase in coastal and oceanic waters, lasting up to 5 years of ocean life, and 3) a final adult return migration phase to natal streams, spanning the last 4–10 months of ocean life.','2026-07-04');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(15,1,'A contributing factor to the complexity of ocean distribution of Chinook salmon is their association with colder temperatures (to 1°C) and deeper depths compared to the other species of Pacific salmon','2026-07-04');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(16,1,'Chinook Salmon may migrate only 100 km but others have been observed 2,500 km northward and/or westward, and are often prevalent in the coastal waters around the rim of the Gulf of Alaska from Southeast Alaska to the Aleutian Islands chain. The return migration phase of maturing Chinook Salmon is often the most rapid, sometimes lasting only weeks.','2026-07-04');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(17,1,'It is noteworthy that extreme ocean migrations are often mirrored in stocks with extensive up-river adult spawning migrations, often to interior or headwater locations within large river system','2026-07-04');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(18,1,'sub-yearling Chinook juveniles remain in proximity to their natal river (migration ranges from 100 to 300 km) during their first summer and fall, then move northward during the winter and following spring.','2026-07-04');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(19,1,'The majority of Asian Chinook mature at ages 1.2, 1.3, or 1.4','2026-07-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(20,1,'Juvenile chinook in coastal asia stay aggregated in small groups and forage with Coho salmon. Once they move offshore they form dense aggregations. ','2026-07-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(21,1,'Asian Chinook migrate to the Pacific Ocean when temperatures drop to 7C','2026-07-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(22,1,'Russian stocks of Chinook salmon dominate the west pacific. But the west and central bering sea are dominated by Alaska origin Chinook salmon.','2026-07-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(23,1,'There are no principle differences in the diets of juvenile, immature, or mature Chinook salmon in russia. They all eat fish, squids and euphausiids. For squid they primarily eat species of the family Gonatidae. ','2026-07-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(24,1,'The commander islands and the north kurile islands are spawning areas for squids. ','2026-07-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(25,1,'Larger asian chinook mainly occur in the bottom and near bottom trawl catches where adult squid Berryteuthis magister, predominate in the Chinook diet','2026-07-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(26,1,'Results for the southwest Bering Sea in June–July showed an average infection rate of 49% (incidence of any lice on a fish) and an intensity or load of 2.1 lice per fish. In the northwest Pacific Ocean in June through August, the average infection rate was 92.8% with an average load of 7.0 lice per fish','2026-07-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(27,1,'scale pattern analyses can differentiate Chinook from eastern and western Kamchatka with 94–95% accuracy.','2026-07-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(28,1,'Chinook Salmon smolts migrate into the eastern Bering Sea primarily from three large river drainages in western Alaska: the Yukon, Kuskokwim, and Nushagak rivers','2026-07-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(29,1,'the average run size of Chinook Salmon in the Yukon River is approximately 236,000 based on the average run of 118,000 Canadian-origin Chinook. The Kuskokwim River supports an average run of 259,000 Chinook Salmon and the Nushagak River supports an average run of 236,000 Chinook Salmon','2026-07-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(30,1,'Chinook Salmon production tends to reflect the amount of suitable spawning and rearing habitats, not river size.','2026-07-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(31,1,'Chinook Salmon in the Yukon River potentially undergo the longest salmon migration in the world up to 3,200 km including passing the Whitehorse Rapids dam','2026-07-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(32,1,'Chinook Salmon from Western Alaska begin their up-river spawning migration shortly after river ice break-up in the spring, typically late May, and peak migration generally occurs in June. Spawning migration (run timing) tends to occur slightly earlier in the Nushagak and Kuskokwim rivers than the Yukon River. Entry of Chinook Salmon into the Yukon River is stock structured, with stocks from the upper drainage arriving earlier than stocks from the lower drainage','2026-07-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(33,1,'Migration rates of Chinook Salmon are generally slower in the Kuskokwim and Nushagak rivers than the Yukon River. Chinook Salmon migration rates of 19–25 km/day are typical for the Kuskokwim River and 11–15 km/day are typical for the Nushagak River','2026-07-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(34,1,'Chinook Salmon reach the largest size and have the highest fecundity of all species of Pacific salmon. Egg size of Chinook Salmon is also relatively large and they have the largest emergent fry of Pacific salmon species','2026-07-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(35,1,'Western Alaska Chinook Salmon typically mature as age-1.2, 1.3 and 1.4, and females tend to mature later than males, characteristics that are consistent with other Chinook Salmon stocks in Alaska','2026-07-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(36,1,'Chinook Salmon from the Upper Yukon River tend to mature later than Nushagak and Kuskokwim River Chinook Salmon, which most likely reflects the higher energetic demand required to reach the upper Yukon River drainage.','2026-07-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(37,1,'Juvenile western alaskan Chinook begin to enter marine habitats in June and reach an average length of 87 mm fork length (FL) within Kuskokwim Bay. Juveniles grow rapidly during their initial marine period and approximately double in length within the first few months at sea reaching an average length of 190 mm to 220 mm FL, by about the end of August. Juvenile Chinook Salmon are distributed within shallow water (<50 m) habitats of the eastern Bering Sea shelf throughout most of their first summer at sea','2026-07-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(38,1,'Juveniles migrate against the northward flowing coastal currents to exit the northern Bering Sea shelf prior to the formation of winter sea ice. Juvenile Chinook Salmon tend to maintain a shallower distribution than Pink or Chum Salmon in the northern Bering Sea','2026-07-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(39,1,'Juvenile Chinook Salmon primarily feed on larval and juvenile stages of fish, predominately Capelin (Mallotus catevarius), Arctic Sandlance (Ammodytes hexapterus), Pacific Herring (Clupea pallasii), and Rainbow Smelt (Osmerus mordax). These prey species are primarily distributed in the shallow/nearshore habitats of the northern Bering Sea','2026-07-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(40,1,'Driftnet fishing for salmon and squid was closed in 1993 following the United Nations moratorium on driftnet fishing in international waters.','2026-07-06');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(41,1,'Unlike Chinook, other western alaskan salmon tend to rear in the Gulf of Alaska as opposed to the bering sea. ','2026-07-06');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(42,1,'Western Alaskan chinook account for around 80% of the driftnet fish catches in the Bering Sea vs 30% in the North Pacific. ','2026-07-06');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(43,1,'Western Alaskan Chinook contribute to the southern shelf highest in the winter and least in the summer. North pacific stocks were at their highest in summer and limited largely to the southern shelf. ','2026-07-06');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(44,1,'Yukon river chinook salmon tend to be distributed farther north than other western alaskan stocks of chinook salmon. ','2026-07-06');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(45,1,'The eastern Bering Sea cold pool is a persistent feature of the northern region of the eastern Bering Sea shelf that forms as a bi-product of winter sea ice','2026-07-06');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(46,1,'Western Alaska Chinook Salmon may utilize the northwest flowing currents along the eastern Bering Sea shelf break as a migratory corridor in the Bering Sea, particularly as age 1.1','2026-07-06');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(47,1,'Yukon River Chinook Salmon transition to piscivory in freshwater at approximately 85–90 mm. Approximately 90% of the diet of juvenile Chinook Salmon in the Yukon River delta consists of fish during their early seaward migration. Chinook Salmon remain predominately piscivorous throughout their first summer at sea from the onset of their marine residency in June','2026-07-06');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(48,1,'Capelin is the primary food source for juvenile Chinook in the northern bering sea whereas sand lance and walleye pollock are more important for southern eastern bering sea chinook (although sand lance is consistent in the diet of both regions). In all cases they are primarily feeding on Age-0 fish. ','2026-07-06');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(49,1,'The lack of Pacific Herring in the diet of juvenile Chinook Salmon may reflect the ability of Pacific Herring to outgrow the prey size window available to juvenile Chinook Salmon in the northern Bering Sea.','2026-07-06');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(50,1,'Invertebrate species are also an important component of juvenile Chinook Salmon diets in the northern Bering Sea, particularly within shallow habitats of Norton Sound. Invertebrate prey primarily consist of large invertebrate species such as crab megalops (Chinocetes sp.), and shrimp (Pandalus sp.); however species composition is variable and can include freshwater insects.','2026-07-06');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(51,1,'Immature Chinook Salmon predominately forage on fish and squid on the eastern Bering Sea shelf and in offshore habitats of the Bering Sea basin. Euphausiids also are an important prey species of Chinook Salmon in the Bering Sea basin','2026-07-06');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(52,1,'Squid were the largest component in the winter diet of Chinook Salmon. The combined contributions of Berrytheuthis magister and Gonatopsis borealis accounted for 74%–82% of the stomach content weight','2026-07-06');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(53,1,'Growth in juvenile Chinook salmon in the northern bering sea seems to peak in mid July ','2026-07-06');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(54,1,'Bioenergetic models indicate that temperature has significant effect on juvenile Chinook salmon growth rates','2026-07-06');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(55,1,'Although mortality during the juvenile life-history stage of Yukon River Chinook Salmon is size-selective, the average size of juveniles is not correlated with survival or adult returns','2026-07-07');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(56,1,'Tagging studies have suggested 40% salmon shark mortality of Chinook salmon in the Bering sea. ','2026-07-07');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(57,1,'When Ichthyophonus infections become clinical, parasite loads in muscle tissue and internal organs are visible to the naked eye, muscle tissue softens, and Chinook Salmon develop a distinctive odor','2026-07-07');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(58,1,'Chinook Salmon in the marine waters of southeast Alaskan (SEAK) archipelago and farther north and west along the coast of the Gulf of Alaska originate from hundreds of streams and rivers from Alaska south to California.','2026-07-07');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(59,1,'Diet studies for Chinook Salmon in SEAK support the characterization that Chinook are predominately piscivorous in coastal habitats. Primary prey species included Capelin, Sand Lance, and Pacific Herring; although a wide variety of other fishes and invertebrates were also observed.
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(60,1,'more euphausiids were observed in juvenile SEAK Chinook Salmon in inlets, and euphausiids increased as a proportion of the diet of juvenile Chinook Salmon each month from June through September','2026-07-07');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(61,1,'Information on diets of larger immature and maturing Chinook Salmon (ocean-ages 2+) has been derived from samples from the commercial troll fishery in inside and outside waters of SEAK. Early studies showed that Pacific Herring was the most important prey species overall, comprising 60–68% of the stomach contents by volume, with Sand Lance the second most frequent prey. They get access to herring because of their larger size. ','2026-07-07');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(62,1,'Squid was more common in the diets of Chinook Salmon caught in more inside waters. Similarly, logbook records from SEAK commercial trollers have documented that Sand Lance and Pacific Herring are the major prey items observed in Chinook Salmon caught, with Pacific Herring predominating in fish taken in inside waters and Sand Lance predominating in fish taken in outside waters (this is all for age 2+ fish)','2026-07-07');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(63,1,'Chinook Salmon originating in SE Alaska rivers are predominately yearling smolts. Peak smolt outmigration occurs in early May and smolt sizes range from 54 to 120 mm, and average ~74 mm length and 4 gm. By late June, these 1.0+ Chinook sampled in SEAK marine waters (1997 to 2000) averaged 136 mm and 28 gm and 171 mm and 56 g, in inshore and strait habitats respectively. By September in these years, ocean-age zero Chinook Salmon averaged 210 mm and 114 g and 269 mm and 282 g in inshore and Icy Straits habitat respectively.','2026-07-07');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(64,1,'Wild stocks in SEAK on average had similar or higher marine survivals than hatchery stocks over the same time periods, even though wild smolts are substantially smaller at entry to the marine environment than hatchery smolts','2026-07-07');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(65,1,'by their second ocean summer SEAK Coho Salmon are three times larger than the same ocean-age SEAK Chinook Salmon.','2026-07-08');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(66,1,'Major large predators of SEAK Chinook salmon are resident killer whales, sea lions, and salmon sharks','2026-07-08');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(67,1,'During spring and summer, upper Columbia River yearling Chinook (spring runs) dominate catches of juvenile Chinook Salmon on the continental shelf, presumably because local stocks are still in the nearshore environment. by fall, Columbia River stocks have mostly been replaced by local stocks, which then continue to dominate catches throughout the winter','2026-07-08');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(68,1,'Relatively few juvenile Salish Sea Chinook Salmon are caught on the continental shelf off British Columbia until their first winter at sea, suggesting that they reside in the Strait of Georgia and Puget Sound for an extended period of time','2026-07-08');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(69,1,'In British Columbia Upon ocean entry, juvenile Chinook Salmon feed on a mixture of zooplankton and insects, feeding primarily on decapods, euphausiids, and hyperiid amphipods during summer','2026-07-08');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(70,1,'In british columbia Chinook salmon become primarily piscivorous around 50–100 g or 160–200 mm','2026-07-08');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(71,1,'The salmon louse is a common parasite frequently observed in wild and farmed salmonids. The prevalence of lice infection tends to be lower in coastal British Columbia compared to Alaska','2026-07-08');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(72,1,'There is consistently low survival in west coast vancouver island chinook salmon associated with el nino events. ','2026-07-08');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(73,1,'‘Salish Sea’ represents the combined areas from the western margin of the Strait of Juan de Fuca, all of Puget Sound, and north to Desolation Sound in the Strait of Georgia.','2026-07-08');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(74,1,'Salmon utilize estuaries for physiological acclimation, initial foraging in marine conditions, and for refuge from predators.','2026-07-08');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(75,1,' juvenile Chinook transiting contaminated estuaries in Puget sound had a 45% lower survival rate than Chinook that did not emigrate through contaminated sites. Contaminated here means contamination by industrial sites. ','2026-07-08');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(76,1,'Diets of Chinook salmon in estuaries with the smallest wetland losses included extensive feeding on mayflies, stoneflies, and caddis flies ... insects all associated with freshwater habitats.','2026-07-09');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(77,1,'In puget sound the onset of piscivory by habitat type is 70mm for inshore and 130mm for offshore','2026-07-09');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(78,1,'The pattern of early marine survival for Chinook salmon in the salish sea is largely determined by conditions in proximity to their source river/estuary, particularly for sub-yearlings.','2026-07-09');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(79,1,'Based on trawl surveys in the Salish sea it seems predation is not the primary mechanism that regulates salmon abundance during the early marine phase','2026-07-09');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(80,1,'Under one diet scenario, resident Chinook predation during June-August could impose 49–59% mortality on the annual cohort of juvenile Chinook entering Puget Sound. That''s a lot of cannibalism. ','2026-07-09');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(81,1,'In the Salish sea the primary marine mammal predators of Chinook salmon are harbor seals and killer whales. Currently, the Strait of Georgia has the highest density of Harbor Seals in the world.','2026-07-09');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(82,1,'There''s a “highly specialized and previously undocumented nocturnal foraging behaviour in which Harbor Seals congregated at night beneath two bridges that spanned the Puntledge River.” Their investigations determined that a small group of seals had learned this foraging behavior, but these seals were highly effective, consuming an estimated 14.5% of the Chum (averaging 140,000 chum fry per night) and 15% of the Coho Salmon (13,000 smolts per night) during their downstream migration','2026-07-09');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(83,1,'The predation of Chinook salmon by pinnepeds in the Salish sea is estimated to be equal to that by killer whales.','2026-07-09');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(84,1,'In the summer in the Salish sea Killer Whale diets are dominated by Chinook salmon (85% of salmon consumed). Chum salmon also get eaten regularly and they are also large, high lipid fish. ','2026-07-09');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(85,1,'While regional differences exist between the diets of juvenile Chinook Salmon from along the CCS, these differences are attributable to variability in the forage base and environmental conditions. ','2026-07-10');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(86,1,'Juvenile fish in the California current system start by eating invertebrates and then switch to piscivory later as they move offshore (where they eat juvenile rockfish and anchovy) ','2026-07-10');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(87,1,'Chinook Salmon juveniles may increase feeding intensity by using behaviors that maximize their interaction with prey such as following evolutionarily embedded navigational cues, tracking increasing gradients of prey resources, or residing at or near oceanographic fronts','2026-07-10');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(88,1,'The notion that CCS Chinook salmon use fronts is supported statistically significant relationship between salmon production and the probability of frontal development during the first few months at sea within the Gulf of the Farallones. Farther north in the Columbia River plume Chinook Salmon concentrate around salinity fronts, where prey was disproportionally more abundant in these frontal regions','2026-07-10');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(89,1,'Off Oregon and Washington, it has been demonstrated that Chinook Salmon juveniles behave in such a way as to reduce southerly transport or advection off shore, therefore, increasing the probability of remaining on a productive shelf to feed.','2026-07-10');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(90,1,'In more open waters of the northern California Current, squid represent a greater proportion of the adult salmon diet. As the fish return to their northern California Current natal region, in preparation to spawn, their diet is largely represented by California sardines (Sardinops caeruleus), Pacific Herring, Northern Anchovy, and euphausiids','2026-07-10');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(91,1,'The timing of upwelling in California can help or harm juvenile survival depending on whether enough time has transpired to setup the appropriate productivity. (Approximately 3 months) ','2026-07-10');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(92,1,'Along the California Current System (CCS), survivals of Chinook Salmon populations covary at a scale of approximately 700km; nearly the same spatial coherence of upwelling','2026-07-10');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(93,1,'In the california current system, predators that may have the greatest impact include piscivorous birds in the estuary and at sea, predatory fish, and, to a degree, marine mammals.','2026-07-10');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(94,2,'The tidal stresses larvae experience near the seafloor is akin to hurricane force winds twice a day.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(95,2,'The boundary layer near the seafloor is really only 100$\mu$m thick so larvae near the seafloor typically use a "balloonist technique" where they rise into the current and then drop as a way to sample the seafloor.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(96,2,'The diffusion limitation of metabolism is the fact that unless action is taken metabolism becomes completely limited by the rate of molecular diffusion (which is extremely slow)','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(97,2,'One strategy to combat the diffusion limitation in plankton is to sink and the other is to swim.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(98,2,'At scales larger than the depth of the water, the homogeneity assumptions implicit in a lot of fluid mechanics are no longer true and motions (like waves) become two dimensional eddies sometimes called geostrophic turbulence.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(99,2,'The Reynolds number is the ratio of inertial forces to viscous forces - $Re=ud/v$ where $u$ is the velocity, $v$ is the kinematic viscosity, and $d$ is a typical dimension.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(100,2,'Molecular diffusion is very slow. Requiring something like 10s to produce an effect over 100$\mu$m','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(101,2,'The Kolmogoroff length scale is given by $2\pi (v^3/\epsilon)^{1/4}$ where $v$ is the kinematic viscosity and $\epsilon$ is the turbulent energy dissipation. And it represents the distance across the smallest eddies (below which viscosity smooths everything out). These lengths typically end up being in the tens of millimeters.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(102,2,'Eddy diffusion is special because unlike molecular diffusion it is the same for all properties - for example salt and heat.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(103,2,'As the velocity of an organism decreases its boundary layer thickens.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(104,2,'There are two kinds of drag, skin drag (or viscous drag), which is caused by friction of the boundary layer and then pressure drag which is generated by the need to move water out of the way and then back into place when moving through it.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(105,2,'Most phytoplankton are denser than water. For example diatoms are more than twice as dense as water.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(106,2,'For organisms in the 1-10 $\mu$m range water diffusion creates faster movement of materials into the cells than movement would allow so they gain nothing by sinking or swimming beyond moving to areas that have higher densities of nutrients.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(107,2,'Larger organisms have more and more advantage from movement. Whereas at 10$\mu$m the benefits are near negligible, swimming when you are around 50$\mu$m in diameter can double or triple nutrient uptake - helping offset the diffusion limitation quite a bit.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(108,2,'Being a small creature at low reynolds number requires specialized feeding techniques such as the "fling and clap"!','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(109,2,'Copepods have been shown to capture glass particles that were too large to digest so long as the chemical cues are there showing that chemoreception is quite important to them. Likewise they also use mechanoreception and can sense moving bodies 3-4 body lengths away.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(110,2,'Due to the fluid dynamics at small reynolds numbers copepods can capture items smaller than the filaments in their sieves.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(111,2,'For benthic plants, water moving across them is essential to breaking the diffusion limitation and places have been shown to increase the uptake of phosphorus in response to stronger currents. Kelps have been shown to asymptotically increase photosynthesis in increasing currents and this might suggest that cutting kelp to increase flow through their forests could be a good management strategy.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(112,2,'The hollow floats on many seaweeds help create movement by taking advantage of turbulence in the water.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(113,2,'There are limits to the strength of currents that help benthic plants as opposed to harm them. In extreme currents or turbidity plants have been shown to have to morphologically change (streamline) in order to not get torn up. Shelter can be very valuable.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(114,2,'In bottoms with very low currents deposit digesting animals are king. As the current picks up you get more and more filter feeders until finally its too fast. Here again we see the impact of the diffusion limitation of metabolism.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(115,2,'Bivalves and other filter feeders have been shown to completely exhaust the resources around them if there isn''t a decent current moving by. Communities change depending on the turbulence and water flow of the habitat.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(116,2,'In the boundary layer near the bottom the differential settling velocities of particles creates a natural sorting effect where organics are in higher concentration above the bottom than inorganic particles.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(117,3,'The level of quick nutrient change in the open ocean is called the nutricline','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(118,3,'The intensity of light at wavelength $\lambda$ at depth $z$ is given by $I=I_o e^{-\alpha_{\lambda}z}$ where $I_o$ is the intensity at the sea surface and $\alpha_{\lambda}$ is the absorption coefficient.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(119,3,'Blue light is absorbed least quickly by the ocean.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(120,3,'The energy used to mix the water column becomes a kind of potential energy in the water column.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(121,3,'Nutrients do not necessarily need to be mixed into the mixed layer given that some recycling can occur. But eventually this process ends as it is unstable for example nutrients do fall through the pycnocline and fish come, eat things, and then swim away removing nutrients from the area.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(122,3,'Recycled nitrogen shows up in the form of ammonia whereas that that comes from below shows up as nitrate (usually). Therefore one can get a sense of how much of the production is recycled vs new by looking at the ratio of these compounds.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(123,3,'Along the equator the east to west flow means that the mixed layer in the west is deeper than that in the east. Because it comes from the eastern mixed layer it is also somewhat nutrient depleted in the west meaning that there is generally more primary productivity along the equator in the east.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(124,3,'When a parcel of water near the equator initially upwells it is high in nutrients but low in phytoplankton. By the time the food web has established itself it has moved west and spun away from the equator. This is what gives the "plume" along the equator its distinctive form.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(125,3,'There is seasonality in the productivity along the equator. This comes from slackening trade winds in February through May. The slackening winds create high surface temperatures and reduced upwelling leading to the minimum phytoplankton production. The winds then pick up through June-July allowing for a lot of upwelling and a peak in productivity in September - November.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(126,3,'The Peru upwelling system has an order of magnitude less productivity than the eastern pacific equatorial upwelling system. This is because while peru''s productivity is very high per unit area, the equatorial upwelling system has an absolutely enormous area.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(127,3,'During warmer months there is warming during the day and cooling at night with a net warming effect. If there are lower winds then this warming will happen faster as the top meters where radiation is absorbed will constitute a greater portion of the mixed layer.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(128,3,'The Gran effect is where (1) turbulent mixing brings nutrients up from the water column, (2) stratification sets in and keeps phytoplankton near the sun and then (3) a bloom sets in.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(129,3,'The compensation depth is the depth at which productivity from photosynthesis is balanced by respiration.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(130,3,'Assuming that plankton get mixed through the whole mixed layer one can compute the critical depth which is the depth of the water column at which overall respiration and photosynthesis balance.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(131,3,'The critical depth is found with the equation $I_c D_{cr} = I_0 (1-e^{\alpha D_{cr}})/\alpha$ where $I_c$ is illumination at the compensation depth, $D_{cr}$ is the critical depth, $I_0$ is the illumination at the surface and $\alpha$ is the absorption coefficient. This is derived by equating the integral of respiration to the integral of photosynthesis throughout a round through the mixed layer.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(132,3,'As the spring bloom progresses it quickly depletes the dissolved nitrogen in the water column, ending the bloom.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(133,3,'As a bloom progresses, the species distribution will often shift toward species with flagella as these have better surface to volume ratios and can swim out of low nutrient concentrations.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(134,3,'An autumn bloom can occur when increasing winds entrain nutrients from the depths due to increased mixing. This depends on latitude - in polar regions the spring and autumn blooms often become one single summer bloom.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(135,3,'During the summer primary production is still high but zooplankton are eating the phytoplankton biomass nearly as fast as it is produced.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(136,3,'One reason the chlorophyll maximum tends to occur at deeper and deeper depths in the open ocean as summer progresses is because there is a decrease in turbulent diffusion with depth. This in turn means the mixed layer up top gets depleted real fast forcing high productivity further down towards the pycnocline where nutrients are cycled up through.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(137,3,'Given solar heating can create a shallowing of the mixed layer (due to surface heating) there is a mixed layer up top, a diurnal thermocline (during the day), a seasonal thermocline and then the interior of the ocean. This creates dynamism in the layering of the open ocean even during the course of a single day.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(138,3,'In the open ocean transient patches of high surface chlorophyll are usually due to episodic upwelling events as phytoplankton are usually quite good at depleting the surface waters that are far from the nutricline.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(139,3,'Another aspect that drives the chlorophyll maximum down is that fact that with less sunlight phytoplankton just grow slower. So there are more nutrients and less of a "boom" capability that would allow them to deplete the nutrients. Things are, in a sense, more balanced.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(140,3,'Given the chlorophyll maximum happens at greater and greater depths as summer goes on and summer is delayed as you go to the poles the chlorophyll maximum seems to rise as you go north.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(141,3,'Internal waves and jets meso-scale fronts can create the episodic upwelling needed to create temporary surface plankton blooms in the open ocean.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(142,3,'In a belt around Antarctica there is upwelling due to the western winds and coriolis force driving water down close to Antarctica and the eastern winds sending water northwards. The boundary creates a divergence that produces the upwelling creating a hotspot for productivity.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(143,3,'Zookplankton are very different in their distribution as compared to phytoplankton because they have the freedom to make extensive vertical migrations. So during low productivity periods they may be keeping cool at depth and then when productivity picks up swim up near the surface.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(144,3,'In stable oligotrophic tropical systems zooplankton are found in the mixed layer and tend to be situated near the thermocline. Where they drop off in abundance is sometimes call the planktocline.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(145,3,'There can be exceptional changes in the number of zooplankton over the course of the year. For example south of Iceland the proportion of copepodites changed from 1-65% between late March and early May.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(146,4,'An estuary is a semi-enclosed coastal body of water which has a free connection with the open sea and within which sea water is measurably diluted with fresh water.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(147,4,'Estuarine circulation is where fresh water flows out on top of a deeper undercurrent of salt water that brings water into the estuary. This flow is usually referred to as buoyancy driven.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(148,4,'If you could pause estuarine circulation before it made its way out to see the freshwater, being lighter, would pile higher allowing for a higher top that then can flow out over the salt water. However as it flows on out turbulence mixes it meaning the layer becomes more saline and thicker as you move away from the coast.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(149,4,'The Richardson number is a ratio of forces that tend to keep internal waves stable - for example differences in density across a boundary - to forces that destabilize it - for example shear forces across the boundary. So as the Richardson number gets low internal waves destabilize and create a lot of internal turbulence.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(150,4,'In some estuaries if the river flow is strong enough the river can push salt water out away from the coast. Depending on the river strength this wedge of freshwater can get farther out or closer to shore. Flocculation and sinking of heavy particles happens at the salinity boundary and so deposition follows the edge of the wedge over the course of time. This creates the wide deposition zones in some estuaries.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(151,4,'In some estuaries tidal forces are so strong they totally mix the water and classic estuarine circulation is just not present at all. Instead you get a gradient of salinity from the land to the sea.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(152,4,'Another type of estuary is the fjord where you have a sill followed by a deep basin. New deep cold, saline water only enters when there is enough activity of the right kind to force water over the sill. Freshwater typically moves over the surface and across the sill.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(153,4,'The processes at the sill in a fjord can be dominated by wind stresses, tidal forces, or simply freshwater runoff.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(154,4,'Thanks to the salt undercurrent and freshwater overcurrent and tidal patterns there are a large variety of currents and countercurrents animals have learned to "ride" to get where they need to be within estuaries.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(155,4,'Fronts are defined as regions with strong gradients.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(156,4,'Fronts are often found where the intensity of turbulent mixing is strong enough to overcome the barrier to mixing presented by stratification. You can approximately calculate this by virtue of asking what the difference in potential energy in the water column is before and after mixing.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(157,4,'Between areas of mixing and stratified zones there is often a transition zone where the difference between stratification and mixing is determined by the strength of the lunar tidal force (i.e. the position in the lunar cycle)','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(158,4,'If fully mixed areas are in places where the water is relatively shallow, then material picked up from the benthos provides a nutrient boost that can overcome the losses to being below the euphotic zone for a bit and allow for nearly year round blooms. However production still varies throughout the year due to solar inclination changes.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(159,4,'In tidally mixed waters there is a net slow down in warming up because stratification cannot set in. Temperature helps with zooplankton growth so zooplankton typically increase in population more slowly in tidally mixed waters.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(160,4,'A river plume is one where surface outflow comes directly from a river. An estuarine plume is one that leaves an estuary and therefore has been mixing with salt water.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(161,4,'Plumes change direction based on currents, wind stress, tidal forces, and coriolis forces. All in all they can change position repeatedly over the day.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(162,4,'River runoff is another source of nutrients and often the peak in run-off coincides with a diatom bloom near the river mouth.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(163,4,'Estuary plumes, unlike river plumes, have had some of their nutrients removed by productivity in the estuary. However the loads of nutrients are often so high due to human impacts that there''s still plenty to go around when they get to the sea. Also the currents in an estuary entrain nutrients in the outgoing water.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(164,4,'Fresh water is lighter than salt water and so can create an earlier set up of stratification. This can lead to earlier blooms.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(165,5,'Upwelling in western regions does happen but due to the thicker mixed layer brings up far fewer nutrients.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(166,5,'Five major coastal currents associated with upwelling regions - California current, Peru current, Canary current, the Benguela current, and the Somali current.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(167,5,'Upwelling generates a pressure gradient and at depth that pressure gradient pushes offshore (rather than inshore near the surface). Due to coriolis this means a poleward current.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(168,5,'Mountains close to the coast can create a preferred location for upwelling due to directing winds toward the coast and thereby intensifying them.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(169,5,'During an El Nino the mixed layer by Peru is 100m deep and so the upwelling actually doesn''t reach below the pycnocline this is part of why productivity becomes so poor.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(170,5,'Upwelling is inferred to be in progress when the isopycnals next to a western coast slope upwards.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(171,5,'Zooplankton are decoupled from primary production because it takes species like copepods weeks to complete a lifecycle rather than days.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(172,5,'In the canary current upwelling system because phytoplankton respond so quickly compared to zooplankton they seem to exceed the grazing capacity of the zooplankton and so those zooplankton are not food limited. Similarly zooplankton provide more food than the fish can actually eat. So no one is food limited.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(173,5,'The heavy dominance of clupeids is a common characteristic of major upwelling systems worldwide.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(174,5,'In the canary current upwelling system there is a shoreward undercurrent that transports and upwells recycled nutrients back into the system - creating a kind of nutrient trap.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(175,5,'In the peruvian upwelling system the upwelling only really extends between 30-80m meaning that in many places the water near the bottom is quite still and there is an accumulation of organic matter.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(176,5,'Along the peruvian upwelling system you can find four zones as you move from the coast, initial upwelling, development of phytoplankton, rapid nutrient depletion, and final nutrient depletion.  This is of course because of the movement of water away from the shore as the food web sets itself up.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(177,5,'During warm years (El Nino) it seems that anchoveta try to find cooler water to hide away in. However that water is often very poor in nutrients for them.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(178,5,'Bakun calculated wind stress using the formula $\tau = \rho_a C_d v^2$ where $\rho_a$ was the density of the air and the drag coefficient $C_d$ was taken to be a constant 0.0026. He then computed the ekman transport $M$ from the wind stress $\tau$ and the coriolis parameter $f$ according to $M=\tau/f$.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(179,5,'There is strong downwelling in Oregon and Washington during the winter.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(180,5,'In upwelling systems, because upwelling is dependent on the wind speed and direction it changes from day to day.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(181,5,'Sardines and anchovy are the most abundant species in the california current upwelling system.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(182,5,'Sardine and anchovy like to spawn in the california bight where there is much less upwelling and more stratification. This seems to be because the larvae in early days need to eat within the chlorophyll maximum in order to survive which requires the stratification. So in some sense while the upwelling is great for productivity it is terrible for the larvae and so they need a safe place to rear before being able to take advantage of the productivity everywhere else.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(183,5,'Like spawning in the california bight, anchovy spawn in places with good stratification in southern africa in order to allow their larvae to rear where they have access to the right kind of food and conditions. However unlike the california current system where such areas are at the terminus of the upwelling currents, here they are at the beginning and the fish can then use the ''Good Hope Jet'' to advect to areas of lots of upwelling to then recruit into the larger fishery.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(184,5,'Nova Scotia is an example of a western margin area with summer upwelling.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(185,6,'One classification of fronts is into - tidal fronts, shelf break fronts, upwelling fronts, estuarine fronts, plume fronts, and fronts associated with geomorphic features such as headlands, islands, and canyons.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(186,6,'Tidal currents interact with the quickly changing bathymetry at the edge of the continental shelf to create large internal waves that in turn create a front.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(187,6,'Tidal fronts can occur on continental shelves where there is a meeting of stratified and unstratified water. As the lunar cycle progresses the strength of the tidal currents change moving the front around. Thus waters become mixed at certain times and stratified at others providing the perfect opportunity for mixing of nutrients into water.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(188,6,'Boundaries of fronts can also create eddies which in turn create mixing. If this mixing happens in areas that are otherwise stratified this can be a boost to productivity.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(189,6,'Across a front you can have stratified water on one side (with nutrients below the pycnocline) and well mixed water on the other (all the way to near the bottom) this can allow nutrients to sneak underneath the pycnocline on the stratified side up into the mixed layer on the mixed side and thus provide an influx of nutrients.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(190,6,'Fronts are usually a place of convergence, as indicated by a clear sign of them being rubbish and organic debris being "attracted" to them. The convergences usually are thought to occur because currents are not exactly in geostrophic balance.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(191,6,'The convergence at fronts can be a source of nutrient and productivity concentration on its own.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(192,6,'Some studies along fronts have shown that simply mixing the water from each side of the front is enough to create a boost in productivity suggesting that each water mass has something necessary but insufficient on its own for rapid growth.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(193,6,'It is possible that flagellates make use of their locomotory powers to move down through the nutricline to get nutrients and then up back towards the sun on their own power.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(194,6,'In some cases crossing the front between tidally mixed waters and stratified waters comes with a change in the benthic community structure as well.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(195,6,'Shelf break fronts seem to operate by tidal currents encountering sharp changes in bathymetry at the shelf break itself creating the mixing needed to throw nutrients into the water column. What''s interesting is that this happens twice daily unlike tidal fronts which pump nutrients over a period of a fortnight. This allows for a more continuous supply in the case of shelf fronts.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(196,6,'An upwelling front is the interface between the upwelling and the rest of the sea (as eventually the upwelled water can''t push the surface waters any further and downwell). Often times the greatest upwelling productivity is found at these fronts.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(197,6,'At the interface of plumes you get a front that is visible in a change in color of the water (due to salinity changes, usually)','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(198,7,'Many stocks of herring breed where there is significant tidal mixing.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(199,7,'As tidal currents move on and off shallow banks they interact with bottom topography to generate unidirectional currents which form gyres around those banks. The tidally mixed water above this bank plus the gyre is believed to be very good for many fish eggs and larva.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(200,7,'There are four components to tides - lunar semi-diurnal, solar semi-diurnal, luni-solar diurnal, principal lunar diurnal. Therefore tides can go from semi diurnal form through to diurnal form.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(201,7,'Internal waves create divergence and convergence zones given they create rotations throughout their waveforms. At the convergence areas you get an accumulation of biomass that increases surface tension and creates a slick.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(202,7,'A common way of creating internal waves is when water flows over an obstacle. This creates what is called a lee wave. Such an obstacle can be as simple as a shelf break.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(203,7,'The retention hypothesis for fish larvae is the idea that the rectification of currents at banks (and around tidally mixed waters) creates a barrier that holds pelagic larvae roughly in place. However it is also possible that fish lay eggs in these areas because they are downstream of a lot of food and that larvae can move vertically to catch the right currents when needed.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(204,7,'The convergences generated by internal waves (associated with slicks at the surface) can accumulate a tremendous amount of biological material. In one experiment 90% of material was brought near shore from offshore by virtue of these slicks. Organisms may actually make use of these slicks - for example megalops larvae are known to swim to the surface and likely use the slicks to carry themselves inshore.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(205,7,'Islands can also interact with tidal currents to create internal waves that may boost primary productivity.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(206,8,'The air in the trade winds come from equatorial air that rises up, cools and then falls again in the subtropics. These circulations are called Hadley cells.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(207,8,'The westerlies do not stay at a constant latitude but meander north and south in waves that are about 10,000km long. Shifts in position account for differences from average weather in the mid latitudes.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(208,8,'The gulf stream forms about 10 cold core rings a year and around 5 warm core rings but the number of warm core rings is extremely variable. Warm core rings tend to be smaller here.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(209,8,'Some animals use the western boundary jets as a way of quick traveling from warm water (as juveniles) to cold productive areas as adults.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(210,8,'Pink salmon use the alaskan gyre to power their huge migration. Sockeye salmon may make multiple circuits of the alaskan gyre over their time at sea.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(211,8,'An eddy that stays affixed to the current that created it is called a frontal eddy.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(212,8,'Cold core rings in the atlantic can be sites for vigorous blooms thanks to the upwelled cold water.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(213,8,'There''s a group called the Ring Group that did a multidisciplinary study of oceanic rings.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(214,8,'As cold rings move into the sargasso sea they bring with them the micro-fauna of the colder regions. Overtime however the number of animals decreases giving a way to, in some sense, age the ring. Initially these rings also have 50% more productivity than the surrounding sargasso sea. Lots of mesopelagic fishes come along with the cold cores as well.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(215,8,'Warm core rings also seem to have jumps in productivity but the mechanisms for this are not necessarily well understood. One possible answer is that cooling of the surface creates convective mixing that in turn brings up the nutrients needed for the bloom. These blooms also cause an uptick in zooplankton biomass over time in the cores of warm core rings. Note that the fauna in the sargasso tends to get caught up in these rings and move into slope water.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(216,8,'Given that warm core and cold core rings can trap fauna, they can also bring unexpected fauna to cold and warm places respectively and this can have a dramatic effect on the year class survival of larval fish.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(217,5,'The centers of the oceanic gyres are not empty but filled with eddies of varying energy.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(218,5,'The mesopelagic is more or less continuous throughout the world oceans.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(219,5,'Because winter mixing in the North pacific does not extend very far there is continuous productivity, even in winter. This is in contrast to the atlantic where during winter phytoplankton have to adopt strategies to tough it out while the mixed layer is very deep.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(220,9,'Annual changes in the biomass of plankton in California is in large part determined by the volume of the flow. Higher volume - more biomass.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(221,9,'ENSO moves the california current around and affects the aleutian low as well creating dramatic changes in the overall flow patterns such as moving the boundary between alaskan waters and the california current northwards sometimes by 1500km.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(222,9,'In years where the california current is weaker and upwelling is as well, some stocks of fish such as mackerel and hake actually have better recruitment. this is believed to be because fewer larva and juvenile fish get advected offshore. Herring off southeast alaska also tend to have better years in El Nino years likely because it forces water levels up and towards the shore providing better retention as well.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(223,9,'Salmon may have their migration routes also shifted by El Nino conditions. This can either be because more rain means more runoff and high coastal currents and/or because the odd conditions create energetic eddies the fish have to swim around. The research here has been focused on fish migrating through SEAK and around Vancouver island.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "note" VALUES(224,9,'El Nino conditions also bring cold water to Japan meaning several species (including walruses) that normally stay north of Japan will come down south to it during these years.','2026-09-05');
INSERT INTO "placement" VALUES(1,37,1,0);
INSERT INTO "placement" VALUES(2,47,1,0);
